/*!
\page gislib_random_streams Caller-Owned Random Number Streams

\tableofcontents

\section gislib_random_streams_overview Overview

The `G_random_*()` functions give a program random number generators of
its own, objects which the program owns, instead of the one generator
shared by the whole program behind `G_drand48()`, and any number of
independent sequences from one seed. That serves parallel work, the
runs of an ensemble, the components of a model which must each be
reproducible on their own, and library or tool code which must not
disturb the shared generator.

`G_random_seed()` alone gives a caller-owned generator with a single
sequence, the one the shared generator produces for the same seed, and
is useful without any threads: code drawing from it neither moves the
shared generator nor is moved by it. `G_random_seed_stream()` is the
function which gives several sequences. It derives from one seed as
many streams as there are units of work, and each unit draws from a
stream of its own, determined only by the seed, the unit's number and
the number of units. Parallel code which draws this way draws the same
random numbers with one thread as with many, and gives the same result
when the rest of the computation is as independent of the schedule,
which it is when every unit writes only its own output and is not when
threads add into shared accumulators. The shared generator cannot even
give the same numbers: it hands each value to whichever thread asks
first, so a parallel run reproduces neither a serial run nor another
parallel run.

\section gislib_random_streams_ring The ring, streams, parts and stride

All 2^48 states of the generator lie on one cycle: a ring along which
each draw moves the generator by one state, and which closes after 2^48
draws, about 281 trillion. A stream is a stretch of that ring.
`G_random_seed_stream(state, seed, stream, streams)` cuts the ring into
`streams` equal parts, or one part more when that number is even, and
places the state object at the start of part number `stream`. The
length of a part, rounded down to an odd number for the reason given
below, is the stride from the start of one stream to the start of the
next. The function returns how many values the stream can
draw before its values meet another stream's: the stride at most, and
less where a twin at a lag, explained below, comes first.

\image html random_streams.svg

The figure shows the ring as a circle of 2^48 states. The seed,
the red dot at the top, is where stream 0 starts and where
`G_srand48()` starts, and the outer arrow gives the direction of
drawing, one step per draw. Four streams cut the ring into five parts,
since four is even: streams 0 to 3 are the four solid arcs, and the
fifth part, left dashed, is the spare one. The double arrow below the
ring spans one part, the stride. The box on the right stands for a `struct G_random_state`: a
position on the ring, the dot on stream 1 which its arrow points to.

Two state objects seeded with the same seed and the same number of
streams but different stream numbers sit on different parts and never
produce the same values, as long as each draws fewer values than the
returned length. This is how independent objects, which share nothing
while they run, avoid each other: they are connected only through the
layout, that is the seed and the number of streams, and separated by
the stream number. Stream 0 starts where `G_srand48()` starts, so a
single stream reproduces the shared generator.

\subsection gislib_random_streams_odd Why the number of parts and the stride are odd

Two streams whose starts are a multiple of 2^46 steps apart, a quarter
of the ring, are one sequence up to a constant: one stream's values are
the other's plus a constant at every draw. This is a property of the
generator, for which 2^46 steps take any state to that state plus a
constant, so two generators that far apart give values which differ by
that constant, divided by 2^48, for as long as they run.

An even number of parts contains such pairs. Cutting the ring into 4096
parts would put stream 2048 at the half mark, 2^47 steps from stream 0,
and stream 1024 at the quarter mark. With seed 42, stream 0 starts with
0.7445, 0.3427, 0.1111; stream 2048 would start with 0.2445, 0.8427,
0.6111, each value stream 0's plus one half, wrapping past 1, and
stream 1024 with 0.9945, 0.5927, 0.3611, each stream 0's plus one
quarter. Any even number of streams puts stream `streams / 2` at the
half mark, so the ring is cut into one part more when the number of
streams is even; stream 2048 of 4096 then starts with 0.5875, 0.9772,
0.7383.

An odd number of parts is not enough on its own, because the stride is
the period divided by the number of parts, rounded down to a whole
number, and when that number is a power of two, or three times one,
some stream starts exactly at a quarter mark. With 33,554,431 streams,
2^25 - 1, the stride would be exactly 2^23, and stream 8,388,608, which
is 2^23, would start 2^23 strides of 2^23 steps, 2^46 steps in all,
after stream 0, at the quarter mark, giving 0.9945, 0.5927, 0.3611 for
seed 42 again. The library therefore rounds an even stride down to the
odd number below it: the stride for 33,554,431 streams is 8,388,607,
stream 8,388,608 starts 8,388,608 steps short of the quarter mark, and
it gives 0.2474, 0.2308, 0.7298, which are not stream 0's values plus
any constant. The rounding costs one value per part: each stream is
one value shorter than the even stride would make it.

Both rules remove twins at the same draw: the odd number of parts
those which every even count has, and the odd stride those of the
counts whose stride would be a power of two or three times one. Twins
at a lag remain in every layout. With a stride s, write m times 2^46
as q times s plus d, with d from 0 to s - 1, for m = 1, 2 and 3.
Stream j + q is then stream j plus m/4 at lag d: its draw t + d is
stream j's draw t plus m/4. Stream j + q + 1 is the same the other way
round, at lag s - d: its draw t is stream j's draw t + s - d plus m/4.

\image html random_streams_twins.svg

The figure shows the twins at a lag for five streams. The seed, the red
dot, is where stream 0 starts, and the hollow dots are its images: the
states a quarter, a half and three quarters of the turn later, from
which the generator draws stream 0's values plus 1/4, 1/2 or 3/4. Each
image falls inside some stream's part; the quarter image lies d steps
after the start of stream 1 and stride - d steps before the start of
stream 2. Stream 1 drawn past d
draws stream 0's values plus one quarter, and stream 0 drawn past
stride - d draws stream 2's values minus one quarter. The smallest of
the six distances between an image and a stream start is the returned
length, here a quarter of the stride.

In the layout with 33,554,431 streams, 2^46 is 8,388,609 times
8,388,607 plus 1, so stream 8,388,609 of 33,554,431 starts one step
before the quarter mark, and its draw t + 1 is stream 0's draw t plus
one quarter: for seed 42 it gives 0.2500, 0.9945, 0.5927, 0.3611. A
twin at a lag
matters only when a stream is drawn past that lag, because before that
the two streams have no value in common up to the constant. For every
count below about a million the smallest of these lags is a quarter of
the stride within a hundredth, so a twin is reached only by a stream
drawn past a quarter of its length; from a few million streams upwards
the lag can be anything down to a single draw, as in the example. Up
to 2^30 streams, about one count in a hundred has a lag below a
thousand draws. The seeding call returns this smallest lag as the
stream length, so a caller who checks the returned length is protected
from twins as well as from overlap. A single stream reports 2^46
values, a quarter of the period, since after that many draws its own
values repeat plus one quarter.

\section gislib_random_streams_usage Usage

\subsection gislib_random_streams_functions Seeding and drawing

`G_random_seed_stream()` seeds a state object to one stream of a
layout, `G_random_seed()` does the same for a layout with a single
stream, and `G_random_double()` draws the next value in [0, 1) from a
state object. `G_random_skip()` advances a state by a number of draws,
which the last section uses to keep earlier results:

\code
#include <grass/gis.h>

struct G_random_state state;
long long length;
double value;

length = G_random_seed_stream(&state, seed, stream, streams);
value = G_random_double(&state);
\endcode

A `struct G_random_state` is a plain variable, 8 bytes holding a
position on the ring and nothing else: no lock, no link to a thread, no
link to other states. The state is the generator's memory, and the
generator is the state together with `G_random_double()`, which moves
the state one step along the ring and returns the value there. Seeding
resets that memory: `G_random_seed_stream()` puts the state at the
start of the stream whatever it held before, so the same call always
restarts the same sequence.

The program declares one state for every sequence it draws at a time,
one per thread when threads take units of work in turn, one per unit
when units last the whole computation, one per run, and uses each from
one thread at a time. States are disconnected in memory, so drawing
from them needs no synchronization and no thread waits for another.
They are connected mathematically: seeded with the same seed and the
same number of streams, they take disjoint parts of one layout of the
ring, so their values do not collide, their streams do not overlap, and
no stream is another shifted by a constant at the same draw (\ref
gislib_random_streams_odd says what remains at a lag). The stream
number is the number of the unit of work, so the values of a unit are
fixed by the seed and the layout, whichever thread draws them; that is
why a run gives the same result under any schedule.

\subsection gislib_random_streams_unit Choosing the unit of work

The unit of work is what the computation is naturally divided into and
numbered by: a raster row, a point, an object, a group of particles, a
run of a model. The stream number is the unit's number and the number
of streams is the number of units, or an upper bound on it, for
example a capacity known before the units are counted; unused streams
cost nothing but their share of the ring. Which of the two patterns
below applies depends on whether a unit draws all of its values in one
go or in many pieces over time.

A single particle and a group of particles are both possible units.
One stream per particle makes every path reproducible on its own, at 8
bytes of state per particle; one stream per group keeps the states
few, but the particles of a group then share one sequence, so their
order within the group must be fixed as well.

When units are nested, as `runs` runs of `rows` rows each, for example
the members of an ensemble, number them flat: one numbering over all
the rows of all the runs, so that every row of every run gets a part of
its own and the runs need no seeds of their own.

\code
long long streams = runs * rows;
long long stream = run * rows + row;

G_random_seed_stream(&state, seed, stream, streams);
\endcode

Do not derive the runs from consecutive seeds (seed + 1, seed + 2 and
so on): for this generator, the values of such runs step by a constant
from one run to the next at every draw. For a simulation this is the
repetition which matters in practice: a unit which starts each run in
the same place would move each run along a shifted copy of its previous
path.

\subsection gislib_random_streams_once Pattern 1: a unit drawn in one go

When a unit of work is drawn in one go, for example one row of a raster,
one point or one object, each thread owns one state object. When the
thread takes a unit, it seeds its object to that unit's stream, draws
the unit's values, and when it takes the next unit seeds the same object
again to that unit's stream. The object's history does not matter: the
values depend only on the seed, the unit's number and the number of
units. Seed stream 0 once before the parallel region: that rejects a
bad seed or layout with a fatal error before any thread starts, and
returns the length, which is the same for every stream of the layout.

\code
struct G_random_state check;
long long length = G_random_seed_stream(&check, seed, 0, units);

if (length < values_per_unit)
    G_fatal_error(_("Too many random numbers per unit for %lld units"),
                  units);

#pragma omp parallel
{
    struct G_random_state state; /* One state per thread. */

#pragma omp for schedule(dynamic)
    for (long long unit = 0; unit < units; unit++) {
        G_random_seed_stream(&state, seed, unit, units);
        for (long long i = 0; i < values_per_unit; i++)
            result[unit][i] = G_random_double(&state);
    }
}
\endcode

When the draw happens in a function which does not know the unit, for
example a callback of an expression evaluator, keep each thread's state
where that function can reach it, such as in an array indexed by the
thread number, and seed it when the unit starts, as above.

Seeding costs about one hundred nanoseconds, roughly fifty draws, so
this is cheap unless a unit draws only a handful of values; then choose
a larger unit, for example a row rather than a cell. Seeding a thread's
state once and drawing all of its units from that one stream would save
the jumps, but then a unit's values would depend on which thread took
it and on the units that thread drew before, and the result would
change with the thread count and the schedule. The saving is small: for
a row of a thousand cells the jump is a tenth of a percent of the
drawing.

\subsection gislib_random_streams_steps Pattern 2: a unit drawn in pieces over time

When a unit of work draws in many pieces over time, for example a group
of particles advanced through many time steps, or a component of a model
which draws at every step, the unit owns one state object for the whole
computation. The object is seeded once at the start to the unit's
stream, and drawn from at every step by whichever thread handles the
unit in that step; a thread may copy it into a private variable for the
step and copy it back. It is never seeded again, because the stream
must continue from step to step; seeding it again would return it to
the start of its part and repeat the same values in every step. Just as
a row's state is not seeded again between its columns, a group's state
is not seeded again between its steps. Seeding a unit's state at its
first draw instead of up front is the same thing, since the state's
history does not matter; what matters is that it is seeded once. What differs is how long the
unit lasts, and so where its state lives, in the thread's loop or in an
array for the whole computation.

\code
/* Advances one unit by one step, drawing its values with
 * G_random_double(state). */
void advance(long long unit, struct G_random_state *state);

struct G_random_state *states = G_malloc(units * sizeof(*states));

for (long long unit = 0; unit < units; unit++)
    G_random_seed_stream(&states[unit], seed, unit, units);

for (int step = 0; step < steps; step++) {
#pragma omp parallel for schedule(dynamic)
    for (long long unit = 0; unit < units; unit++)
        advance(unit, &states[unit]);
}
\endcode

\section gislib_random_streams_limits Limits and the returned length

The library does not limit how many values a stream draws, and cannot:
it does not know how many values a unit will draw. A stream drawn past
the returned length meets another stream's values plus a constant, and
drawn past the stride it continues into the next stream's part and from
there produces that stream's values, shifted in time. The parts, one
more than the streams when that number is even, times the values each
draws must therefore stay below 2^48, about 2.8e14, and the values each
draws must stay below the returned length, which covers both limits.
Examples:

- a million units drawing a million values each fit: each stream can
  draw 70,191,703 values, about 70 times what it draws;
- ten million units drawing ten thousand values each fit: 4,792,165
  values per stream, about 480 times;
- a billion units drawing ten thousand values each fit narrowly: 14,701
  values per stream;
- a hundred billion units do not fit ten thousand values each: a stream
  can draw only 509.

The length depends on the exact count: ten million and 308 units can
draw only 15,528 values each. Check the returned value rather than
assume it. So one stream per row of a large raster, and one stream per
particle of a simulation with millions of particles, both fit. One
stream per particle for every run of a thousand-run ensemble with ten
million particles, ten billion streams of 4,177 values each, does not;
that is where a generator with a longer period becomes necessary.

Compare the returned length with the most values a unit can draw, not
the average: for a row, the columns times the draws per cell; for a
particle, the time steps times the draws per step. A rejection loop
has no fixed maximum, but a bound is easy: a loop with acceptance rate
p costs 1/p attempts per value on average, and the total over S steps
varies by only about sqrt(S (1 - p)) / p attempts, so twice the
average is a safe bound once S is more than a few dozen. Refuse the
layout with `G_fatal_error()` or warn with `G_warning()` when it does
not fit.

How much an overlap matters depends on the use, and in many uses it
matters little. Where a value's role is fixed by its position, as one
value per cell, a stretch repeated in another stream is a repeated
pattern. Where the values feed a process which has moved on, as the
displacements of a particle over time, a stretch shared with the
neighbouring stream lands at a different time and place, and when the
process draws in pairs the odd stride even puts the neighbour's pairs
between the stream's own pairs. The check is still worth making; what
to do when it fails, refuse or warn, is the tool's decision.

\section gislib_random_streams_quality Quality of the values and how to check it

\subsection gislib_random_streams_generator The generator

All the values here come from the generator behind `G_drand48()`, the
48-bit linear congruential generator of `drand48()`:
the state is a 48-bit integer, a draw replaces it by 0x5DEECE66D times
the state plus 11, keeping the low 48 bits, and the value is the new
state divided by 2^48. The values are multiples of 2^-48, uniform in
[0, 1), and a stream is a stretch of the one sequence the generator has,
so within a stream the values are exactly those `G_drand48()` has always
given GRASS tools, and as good: fine for simulations, sampling and Monte
Carlo estimates, and not for cryptography or for anything where an
observer must be unable to predict the next value from the earlier
ones. As in every linear congruential generator with a power-of-two
modulus, the low bits of a value are weaker than the high bits (the
lowest bit alternates), which is why an integer in [0, n) is taken from
the high bits, by multiplying the value by n as shown at the end of
\ref gislib_random_streams_migrating, and not from the low bits.

A layout does not change the generator; it places the streams along
the one sequence. Where quality can be lost is therefore not within a
stream but between two sequences: two sequences which are one sequence
shifted in value, in time, or both. The relations of that kind the
library knows about follow, each with an example and with the rule
which avoids it. The examples use seed 42, and the table gives the
first four values of the sequences involved.

|                | draw 1 | draw 2 | draw 3 | draw 4 |
|----------------|--------|--------|--------|--------|
| seed 42        | 0.7445 | 0.3427 | 0.1111 | 0.4223 |
| seed 42 + 2^30 | 0.9945 | 0.5927 | 0.3611 | 0.6723 |
| seed 42 + 2^31 | 0.2445 | 0.8427 | 0.6111 | 0.9223 |
| seed 43        | 0.6153 | 0.0473 | 0.8495 | 0.8879 |
| seed 44        | 0.4861 | 0.7519 | 0.5880 | 0.3534 |

\subsection gislib_random_streams_relations Relations between sequences

<b>Seeds 2^31 or 2^30 apart.</b> Two seeds 2^31 apart give sequences which
differ by exactly one half at every draw, and 2^30 apart by one
quarter: seed 42 + 2^31 is seed 42 plus one half and seed 42 + 2^30 is
seed 42 plus one quarter, wrapping past 1. The seed occupies bits 16 to
47 of the state, so such seeds start 2^47 or 2^46 apart in state, and a
draw keeps that difference, since the multiplier is 1 modulo 4. Each
sequence on its own is as good as any other; the two together are one
sequence shifted in value. The rule: seeds used together stay below
2^30, where no two seeds are that far apart (\ref
gislib_random_streams_seeds).

<b>Consecutive seeds.</b> Seeds 42, 43 and 44 give sequences with nothing
in common at first sight, yet the differences 43 minus 42 and 44 minus
43, modulo 1, are the same at every draw: 0.8708, 0.7046, 0.7384,
0.4655 for the first four. Consecutive seeds start 2^16 apart in state,
and after t draws that difference has become the multiplier to the
power t times 2^16, modulo 2^48, whatever the seed. Runs seeded seed,
seed + 1, seed + 2 and so on are therefore not independent runs but a
lattice: at every draw, run k has run 0's value plus k times the step
of that draw, modulo 1. The rule: runs meant to be independent are
streams of one seed (\ref gislib_random_streams_unit).

<b>Two streams of one layout.</b> Two streams of one layout are disjoint
stretches of one sequence: neither contains a value of the other while
each draws fewer values than the returned length, and neither is the
other plus a constant at the same draw, since the number of parts and
the stride are odd. What remains is a twin at a lag, explained in \ref
gislib_random_streams_odd and shown in its figure: past the returned
length a stream's values are another stream's values plus a constant.
With 6,878,864 streams the returned length is 549, and stream 0's
550th value is stream 5,159,149's first value plus one quarter, its
551st that stream's second plus one quarter, and so on, while no such
relation holds at any shorter lag. The rule: draw no more values from a
stream than the returned length (\ref gislib_random_streams_limits).

<b>A stream drawn past its length.</b> Past the returned length a stream
meets a twin, as above, and past the stride it enters the next part and
from there draws exactly the next stream's values: with four streams,
stream 0's value number stride + 1 is stream 1's first value. This is
the previous relation seen from the stream drawn too far, and the rule
is the same.

The first two are relations between seeds, and streams of one seed
avoid them, since a layout has one seed. The last two are relations
within a layout, between the streams of one seed, and the returned
length guards them. A tool which derives its sequences as streams of
one seed and draws each within the returned length is clear of every
relation listed here.

\subsection gislib_random_streams_checking How to check it yourself

All the relations above are of one kind, two sequences which are one
sequence shifted in value and possibly in time, and one comparison
finds them. Draw a hundred or so values from each of two sequences, and
then:

1. Compare at the same draw: subtract the values draw by draw and take
   the differences modulo 1. A constant difference means a twin at the
   same draw: the two sequences are one sequence shifted in value, as
   for seeds 2^31 apart. A difference which changes from draw to draw
   but is the same for another pair of seeds the same distance apart
   means a lattice, as for consecutive seeds.
2. Compare at lags: shift one sequence by d draws against the other and
   subtract again. A constant difference at lag d means a twin at that
   lag: one sequence's draw t + d is the other's draw t plus a
   constant.
3. Compare against the returned length: for two streams of one layout,
   no lag below the returned length gives a constant difference,
   whichever two streams are compared, and the returned length is the
   smallest lag at which one can appear.

The differences are exact: every value is a multiple of 2^-48 and a
double holds the difference of two such values without rounding, so the
comparison needs no tolerance, and comparing the states, the values
times 2^48, is the same comparison in integers. The tests in
`lib/gis/tests/lib_gis_lrand48_test.py` are worked examples of the
three steps. `constant_shift()` is the comparison at the same draw and
at small lags, which `test_random_streams_have_no_lockstep_twins` runs
on every pair of streams of small layouts and
`test_random_streams_with_power_of_two_stride_have_no_twins` on the
pair an even stride would make twins;
`test_random_seeds_apart_by_high_powers_of_two_are_shifted` finds the
constants one half and one quarter; `stream_length()` computes the
returned length from the stride alone,
`test_random_seed_stream_returns_stream_length` compares it with what
the library returns, and
`test_random_stream_meets_a_twin_right_after_its_length` draws past the
returned length and finds the twin at that lag and at no shorter one.
`lcg_jump_reference()` there gives the state after any number of draws
in closed form, so a sequence can be placed anywhere on the ring
without drawing its way there.

Standard statistical test batteries, such as TestU01 or PractRand,
assess a single sequence, and they apply to a stream as they apply to
`drand48()`, since a stream is a stretch of the same sequence; they are
not repeated per layout. A layout changes where the streams lie on the
ring, not the values along it, and what it can add is the relations
above, which the comparison finds and which a battery run on one stream
cannot see.

\section gislib_random_streams_seeds Seeds

A seed outside -2^31 to 2^32 - 1 is a fatal error. Within that range,
use seeds from 0 to 2^30 - 1, about a billion; any value a tool's
integer seed option holds below that is fine. Other seeds work, with
two things to know. The generator uses the low 32 bits of the seed, so
-1 and 4294967295 are the same seed. Two seeds exactly 2^31 apart give
sequences shifted by one half at every draw, and 2^30 apart by one
quarter; that is why the recommended range ends at 2^30, below which no
two seeds are that far apart. The range is the requirement, and the
recommendation concerns pairs of seeds: a single seed anywhere in the
range is as good as any other, and only seeds used together, as in a
series of runs, need to stay away from 2^30 and 2^31 apart. Runs meant
to be independent should be streams of one seed in any case:
independent sequences come from the streams of one seed, not from
several seeds, whether those are far apart or consecutive.

A seed option is parsed into a `long long` with `strtoll()`, checking
that the whole string was consumed and that `errno` is not `ERANGE`,
since the parser accepts any digits for an integer option; a `long`
does not hold the range on every platform.

When the user gives no seed, generate one with
`G_random_generate_seed()`. It takes the value of `GRASS_RANDOM_SEED`,
or of `SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH`, from the environment, which scripted and
reproducible runs set, and otherwise uses the time and the process ID;
the result is always in range. Record the seed the tool used, for
example in the history of the output map, so that the run can be
repeated.

\section gislib_random_streams_migrating Migrating from the shared generator

Code which seeds the shared generator with `G_srand48()` and draws with
`G_drand48()` gets exactly the same values from a state seeded with
`G_random_seed()` and drawn from with `G_random_double()`:

\code
/* Before: the shared generator. */
G_srand48(seed);
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
    values[i] = G_drand48();

/* After: a caller-owned generator, with the same values. */
struct G_random_state state;

G_random_seed(&state, seed);
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
    values[i] = G_random_double(&state);
\endcode

Code which generated a seed with `G_srand48_auto()` when the user gave
none calls `G_random_generate_seed()` instead; it returns the same value
without seeding the shared generator.

`G_srand48()` silently reduced any seed to its low 32 bits, while the
caller-owned functions refuse a seed outside their range with a fatal
error. A tool which passes a user's seed through therefore now refuses
seeds it once accepted silently, such as 5000000000. Validate the seed
right after parsing, before any work is done, with `G_random_seed()`
on a local state or with the stream 0 seeding shown in pattern 1.

Existing results are reproduced by a single stream, and only by it: a
layout with several streams changes the values, since only stream 0
continues the shared sequence. Parallel code whose values came from the
shared generator changes them in any case, because they depended on the
schedule. Such code moves to one stream per unit of work, here one per
row, with one state per thread, and its results then depend neither on
the number of threads nor on the schedule:

\code
/* Before: the shared generator; which row gets which values depends
 * on the schedule. */
G_srand48(seed);
#pragma omp parallel for
for (long long row = 0; row < rows; row++)
    for (long long col = 0; col < cols; col++)
        result[row][col] = G_drand48();

/* After: one stream per row, one state per thread. */
#pragma omp parallel
{
    struct G_random_state state;

#pragma omp for
    for (long long row = 0; row < rows; row++) {
        G_random_seed_stream(&state, seed, row, rows);
        for (long long col = 0; col < cols; col++)
            result[row][col] = G_random_double(&state);
    }
}
\endcode

\subsection gislib_random_streams_keeping Keeping earlier results

Where every unit draws a known, fixed number of values, the old
single-threaded results can be kept and still be independent of the
schedule: seed a state and skip the values drawn by the units before
the current one with `G_random_skip()`, and the unit draws the stretch
of the single sequence it drew in the serial run. A row of `cols` cells
drawing one value per cell:

\code
#pragma omp parallel
{
    struct G_random_state state;

#pragma omp for
    for (long long row = 0; row < rows; row++) {
        G_random_seed(&state, seed);
        G_random_skip(&state, row * cols);
        for (long long col = 0; col < cols; col++)
            result[row][col] = G_random_double(&state);
    }
}
\endcode

The skip costs the same as a seeding call, whatever its size. This
packed layout has no spacing between the units, so the number of draws
of every unit must be exact, and a unit drawing more runs into the next
unit's values. It meets no twins as long as the whole sequence stays
below 2^46 draws, a quarter of the period, because all its values then
lie within one quarter of the ring; that number is what
`G_random_seed()` returns, so compare the total of all units with it,
and warn rather than refuse where earlier versions produced the same
values without complaint. Where the number of draws of a unit is not
known in advance, as for a particle whose steps draw a varying number
of values, use streams instead.

\image html random_streams_packed.svg

The figure shows the ring unrolled into a bar from the seed to 2^48 for
four units drawing L values each, in the spread layout of
`G_random_seed_stream()` above and in the packed layout of
`G_random_seed()` with `G_random_skip()` below. Above, the units start
a stride apart, one at the start of each of five parts, and each draws
a short stretch of its part; the rest of the part is spacing. Below,
unit r skips r times L values and draws the next L, so the four
stretches follow each other without a gap, and the run must end before
2^46, the hollow dot, which is the first image of the seed (\ref
gislib_random_streams_odd): from there on the sequence repeats itself
plus one quarter.

The caller-owned API has no integer-returning functions at all. To get
an integer in [0, n), use `(long)(G_random_double(&state) * n)`. With
n = 2^31 this is exactly the value `G_lrand48()` gives at the same draw
of the shared sequence, since the state has 48 bits and a double holds
the product without rounding, and the value of `G_mrand48()` is
`(long long)(G_random_double(&state) * 4294967296.0)` read as a two's
complement 32-bit integer, that is, with 4294967296 subtracted when it
is 2147483648 or more.

*/
